<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Spoleto</h1><p class="st-meta">570 CE – 1198 CE (approx.) · capital Spoleto</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Powerful Lombard duchy of central Italy whose later dukes, Guy and Lambert, became kings of Italy and emperors in the 9th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> warriors under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroald-i-of-spoleto">Faroald I</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> in the Umbrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> around 570, cutting the Byzantine route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Ravenna</a>. The duchy grew into a large territory covering much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, largely independent of the kings in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> acted like rulers in their own right, waging war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and sometimes defying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kings; Transamund II was deposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-king-of-the-lombards">Liutprand</a>. After 774 it passed to Frankish dukes but kept its weight. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-iii-of-spoleto">Guy of Spoleto</a> was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 889 and emperor in 891, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy">Lambert</a> also became emperor. The town's Romanesque churches reflect its medieval wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 10th to 12th centuries the duchy was often held with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a> by imperial appointees. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> in 1155. In 1198 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> took the duchy into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>, where it remained until 1860.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-spoleto.jpg" alt="Duchy of Spoleto" width="320" height="460"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. MapMaster assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_1000_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>570 CE – 1198 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Spoleto</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Faroald I, Guy of Spoleto, Lambert</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombardic, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus Spoletanus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=884&amp;focus=duchy-of-spoleto" aria-label="Duchy of Spoleto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4988 659 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Spoleto lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5308 915L5218 839M5308 915L5218 839M5308 915L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5218 839h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 915h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 915h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Spoleto. <a href="/atlas?year=884&amp;focus=duchy-of-spoleto">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
